### FEATURE_DISCOVERY — the activity a person names, and the step key that follows it
###
### Every request here is a real one against a running instance. Sign in first: the session cookie and
### the cross-site token are what the chain expects. The demo backend runs on 8081 — Tomcat9 owns 8080
### on this machine.
###
### **This file proves one thing: a step can be keyed on what somebody says they did, not only on the
### department they sit in.** A mark has always carried a work type, and a work type is a coarse word —
### CONTENT covers writing a caption, writing an article and writing a script. Every engagement that ran
### those three collapsed them into one step, and a thirteen-activity process came back as five. The
### activity is the finer word, chosen from a list rather than typed fresh, and the requests below walk
### it end to end: what the list holds, adding to it, and carrying one on a mark.
###
### **The list is the whole design, and typing is not.** A free-text box would produce "caption",
### "Caption", "write caption" and "write the caption" inside one week, and four names for one activity
### fragment a process exactly as badly as one name for four activities merges it. So the field offers
### what the workspace already uses, ordered by how often, and adding is the exception rather than the
### path — but adding is never blocked, PRINCIPLE-NEVER-BLOCK, because a person who cannot name their
### own work stops naming it.
###
### **The activity is OPTIONAL, and that is the safety property rather than a compromise.** A mark
### without one keys its step on the work type exactly as every mark did before this column existed, so
### a cold workspace works on day one and resolution improves as people name things. Nothing below
### requires an activity for a mark to succeed; the last request in this file shows what changes when
### one is sent, and the answer is a longer step key and nothing else.
###
### **Nothing here is a figure about a person.** `activity_usage` records who chose what, because a
### suggestion has to be able to say where it came from, and no route aggregates by that column —
### invariant 14, and this is the table it would be easiest to break.
###
### Seed the workspace first, or the list will be empty and the marks will have nothing to attach to:
###   ./mvnw spring-boot:run "-Dspring-boot.run.profiles=demo"

@host = http://localhost:8081
@owner = maria@atelier.ro
@employee = ionut@atelier.ro
@password = password1234

### **CSRF applies to every POST here.** The session is a cookie, so the token travels in a readable
### cookie and must come back in a header (`SecurityConfiguration`, double-submit). A REST client that
### keeps cookies does this for you; `curl` needs `-c`/`-b` and an explicit `X-XSRF-TOKEN`, and without
### it every request answers **401 with an empty body** — which reads exactly like a wrong password and
### is not one.

### Copy real identifiers out of `discovery.http` and out of the POST below into these.
@jobId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@messageId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@performerId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
@activityId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### Sign in as the person who does the work. Every endpoint in this file requires WORK_NODE_MARK, which
### every role holds — naming your own work is recognition rather than classification, and a permission
### gate on it would be a gate on this zone's only supply of evidence.
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{employee}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###############################################################################
### DISCOVERY-MANAGE-ACTIVITIES-01 — what the workspace already says it does
###############################################################################

### 200. Every activity anybody can still pick, ordered by how often it has been used.
###
### **Fetched once, not searched.** A workspace holds tens of these, not thousands, and a debounced
### endpoint behind a text field would spend a network round trip answering what filtering forty rows in
### the browser answers in under a millisecond. The mark panel loads this list and narrows it locally.
###
### Two fields on each row are worth reading rather than skimming. `timesUsed` is why the top of the
### list covers most marks — it is maintained on use rather than counted live, so it is the product's
### own claim about itself. And `departments` names everyone who has used the activity: three or more
### comes back as **`tooGenericToBeOneThing: true`**, because "review" means one thing in Design and
### another in Legal, and an activity broad enough to be picked everywhere has stopped naming anything.
### That flag is shown, never enforced — the workspace is told, and decides.
GET {{host}}/api/discovery/activities

###############################################################################
### Adding one — never blocked, and never a duplicate
###############################################################################

### 201. A name nobody has used here yet.
###
### The response carries the `slug` alongside the name, and the slug is the identity rather than the
### decoration: it is what gets carried inside the step key, which is why its charset is narrower than
### the name's. A colon in a step key forges an output type, a slash a conversation split, a hash a
### concept split and a plus a second step, so normalisation folds case and diacritics and reduces
### everything else to hyphens. "Write the caption" becomes `write-the-caption`.
###
### Copy `id` out of the response into `@activityId` above.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Write the caption"
}

### 201 again — **and the same `id` as the request above.** This is the thing to watch in this file.
###
### The name is cased differently and padded, and it normalises to the slug that already exists, so the
### existing row comes back rather than a refusal or a second row. Compare the two `id` values: they are
### equal, and `timesUsed` has not moved, because naming an activity is not using one.
###
### **Refusing here would be worse than it looks.** The person who typed "write the caption" is not
### making a mistake, they are describing the same work; told the name was taken they would invent a
### third name for it, and the workspace would end the week with two activities where the point of the
### list is that it has one. This is the same reasoning that makes adding a client idempotent by name.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "  write the CAPTION  "
}

### 400 REFUSED, for a name that leaves nothing behind once it is normalised.
###
### `":::"` is three characters that all reduce to separators, so the slug would be empty — and an empty
### slug is not a small problem. Two people naming two different things would both land on it, the step
### keys of unrelated work would collide, and a process would be assembled out of steps that have nothing
### to do with each other. The refusal is in the domain rather than in this endpoint, and it says what it
### is: no two people could ever pick this and mean the same thing.
###
### **This is the one refusal in the file**, and it is not a validation preference. A blank name is
### caught earlier by `@NotBlank`; this catches the name that looks like a name and is not one.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": ":::"
}

###############################################################################
### Carrying one on a mark — the whole reason the list exists
###############################################################################

### 201. The same mark as in `discovery-brackets.http`, with the activity attached.
###
### **Watch the response's `activity` field, and watch what does NOT change.** The activity comes back
### as the name, and `bracketId`, `joined` and `destination` are exactly what they would have been
### without it: the bracket address is chat · client · project · work type · performer, and the activity
### is not one of the five. Marking the same work twice with the same activity still answers
### `"joined": true` on the same bracket. If the activity had reached the address, one engagement's
### thirteen activities would have opened thirteen brackets where the work is one piece, and every
### duration in it would be measured over a fraction of the work.
###
### What it does change is the step key the clusterer reads afterwards. A node with an activity keys on
### `K:CONTENT@write-the-caption`; the same node without one keys on `K:CONTENT`, which is what every
### node did before this column existed.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/work
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "performerId": "{{performerId}}",
  "workType": "CONTENT",
  "activityId": "{{activityId}}",
  "verb": "CREATE"
}

### 201 with `"activity": null`. The ordinary mark, unchanged.
###
### Omitting `activityId` is not a degraded path and there is no nudge for it. This is the request every
### caller in the product made before the field existed, it answers identically, and its node keys on
### `K:CONTENT`. Run this and the one above in the same engagement and read the two step keys side by
### side — that difference, repeated across a quarter of real work, is the whole measurement.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/work
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "messageId": "{{messageId}}",
  "jobId": "{{jobId}}",
  "performerId": "{{performerId}}",
  "workType": "CONTENT",
  "verb": "ADD"
}

### 200 again, and now the activity that was just used has moved.
###
### `timesUsed` is up by one and `lastUsedAt` is set, so the next person opening the mark panel finds it
### near the top. Re-read the list after marking rather than before: the ordering is the only thing that
### makes a list of forty usable without a search box, and it is earned by use rather than configured.
GET {{host}}/api/discovery/activities

###############################################################################
### Two names for one thing — merging, and the one that must never be automatic
###############################################################################

### Name a second activity for work the workspace already has a word for, so there is something to
### merge. Copy its `id` into `@duplicateId`.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Post copy"
}

@duplicateId = 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### 200. "Post copy" becomes "Write the caption", and every node that carried the losing name moves.
###
### **This is the request in the file with the widest blast radius, and it is deliberately manual.**
### It rewrites `work_node.activity_id` and `activity_usage.activity_id` for every node the losing
### activity ever named, which is the point: two half-populated steps become one, and a process that
### came back as two shapes comes back as one. The two use counts add up, so the survivor sorts where
### its real history puts it rather than below activities it has just absorbed.
###
### The losing activity is **kept**, as MERGED with `merged_into_id` pointing at the survivor. Deleting
### it would orphan every step key it ever produced, and the pointer is the only record that a person
### decided these were one thing.
###
### **A wrong merge cannot be undone by another merge.** Two different activities become one and nothing
### afterwards can tell their nodes apart. That asymmetry is why nothing in this product merges on its
### own: character matching in the browser suggests, the model below suggests, and a person presses.
###
### ⚠️ **Watch what does NOT happen to the library.** Templates drafted from the absorbed name keep
### their titles, their approvals and their use counts. Nothing is retired, because
### `DECISION-NO-TEMPLATE-BINDING-01` binds nothing — a person deciding that two words mean one thing
### is not evidence that a template they approved has stopped describing real work. What happens
### instead is that a **finding** is raised, at merge time rather than on the next scheduled run,
### carrying those templates as its evidence: *"2 approved templates still name 'Post copy', which is
### now 'Write the caption'"*, with **Review them** and **They are still correct**. Read it back at
### `GET /api/analysis/findings`. If no analysis has ever run there is nothing to attach it to and
### nothing is raised, which is the one case where the merge is silent.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities/{{duplicateId}}/merge
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "intoId": "{{activityId}}"
}

### 409 ACTIVITY_MERGE_REFUSED. Into itself, or into one that is already merged or retired.
###
### Both refusals protect the same thing. Merging an activity into itself would mark it MERGED and point
### it at itself, leaving a name nobody can pick and nothing to pick instead; merging into a name that
### has already gone would hide both rather than joining them. The refusal names which side it was.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities/{{activityId}}/merge
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "intoId": "{{activityId}}"
}

### 200. Retired — nobody can pick it again, and **nothing already marked with it changes.**
###
### Read those two halves separately, because the second is the one that is easy to get wrong. A step
### key that has been discovered stays the step it was; if retiring rewrote the nodes, every shape built
### on that step would silently become a different shape and the runs before and after would stop being
### comparable. Retiring stops a name spreading. It is not a rewrite of the graph behind it.
###
### **Retire a name that should stop; merge a name that duplicates.** Retiring a duplicate strands its
### nodes under a word no one can choose again, which is the worst of both.
POST {{host}}/api/discovery/activities/{{duplicateId}}/retire
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###############################################################################
### The one place a model belongs in this feature
###############################################################################

### 200, 204, or 409 — and the three answers mean different things.
###
### The browser already catches "caption" against "captions" by comparing characters, instantly and for
### nothing. It cannot catch **"post copy" against "caption set"**, which share none. That is the whole
### job of this endpoint and the reason the plug point exists.
###
### **It suggests. It never merges.** The answer is rendered as the same question a character match
### renders — *this looks like "Write the caption", use it or say yours is different* — and nothing is
### written either way. The verdict is also checked against the candidates that were sent, so a model
### that invents a name it was not offered is discarded rather than shown.
###
### **200** carries the existing activity it considers the same work. **204** is the model having no
### opinion, which is a correct and common answer. **409** is the model being off, unavailable, or this
### plug point not switched on — a different fact, and a caller that could not tell them apart would
### show the person a wrong reason for an empty result.
###
### Requires `flowops.ai.plug.same-activity=true` and the runtime switch on
### (`PUT /api/node-pipeline/ai {"on":true}`). Both, deliberately: one is a deployment decision and the
### other is a demonstration one.
POST {{host}}/api/node-pipeline/activities/near-miss
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "typed": "Post copy",
  "candidates": ["Write the caption", "Shot list", "Schedule the post"]
}

###############################################################################
### What is deliberately not here
###############################################################################

### **There is no route that renames an activity into a different slug.** The domain refuses it: the
### slug is carried inside every step key the activity has ever produced, and reslugging would leave
### two eras of one process looking like two processes. A name may be tidied where it normalises to the
### same slug, and nothing else.
###
### **There is no route that deletes one.** Merge and retire are above and delete is not, because a slug
### is unique across ACTIVE, MERGED and RETIRED alike: reissuing one would collapse two eras of history
### into a single step, and deleting would orphan every step key the activity ever produced rather than
### ending it. The schema enforces the states the two routes move between (`activity_status_is_known`,
### `activity_merged_names_its_survivor`).
###
### **There is no route that reports by who chose what.** `activity_usage.used_by` exists so a
### suggestion can say where it came from, and no endpoint aggregates it. "Caption writing: 40 marks" is
### a fact about the work and is allowed; the same data pointed at a person is forbidden.
###
### **And nothing here reaches a task.** Naming an activity writes a row in this zone and no further —
### the one thing that crosses zones is an approved template, and `NoRouteFromDiscoveryToATaskTest`
### asserts the absence of the rest rather than trusting it.
